/** Generated from the overhaul textbook. Re-run tools/import-overhaul-courses.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"bassstation-level-1","number":1,"title":"Identity, Hardware Architecture, and Power Safety","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Define the Novation Bass Station II as a digitally-controlled analogue monophonic synthesizer with paraphonic capability. - Articulate the technical distinction between analogue audio path generation and digital parameter scanning/storage","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-1-1","code":"1","title":"Identity, Hardware Architecture, and Power Safety","blurb":"- Define the Novation Bass Station II as a digitally-controlled analogue monophonic synthesizer with paraphonic capability. - Articulate the technical distinction between analogue audio path generation and digital parameter scanning/storage","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 1.1 — What This Instrument Is","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Define the Novation Bass Station II as a digitally-controlled analogue monophonic synthesizer with paraphonic capability.","Articulate the technical distinction between analogue audio path generation and digital parameter scanning/storage.","Characterize the single-voice monophonic signal path versus the shared-filter paraphonic operating mode.","Identify the shared documentation lineage across hardware revisions (Bass Station II, AFX Station, Bass Station II Swifty Edition).","Delineate strict operational and functional boundaries between the hardware synthesizer, pure MIDI controllers (e.g., Novation Launchkey series), and standalone software emulations (e.g., GForce Bass Station)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-1-1","title":"1.1.1: Analogue Signal Path with Digital Parameter Control","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Novation Bass Station II is an electronic musical instrument whose primary sound-generating and sound-shaping signal chain is 100% discrete analogue circuitry, coupled with a high-resolution digital control system."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[Digital Control Subsystem]\n  │  Microprocessor / DACs / Digital Scanning\n  ├── Front Panel Potentiometers &amp; Switches (Digital Encoders/ADC)\n  ├── 128-Patch Digital Storage &amp; Recall (0–127)\n  ├── MIDI In/Out &amp; Class-Compliant USB MIDI\n  └── Modulators (Dual Digital LFOs: 0 to ~190 Hz)\n  │\n  ▼ (Control Voltages / Gate Signals)\n[Analogue Signal Path]\n  Osc 1 / Osc 2 / Sub-Osc ──► Mixer ──► Analogue Filter ──► Distortion ──► VCA (Amp) ──► Mono Line Out / Phones\n  (Discrete DCO Architecture) (6 Sources) (Classic LP/BP/HP/Acid) (Pre-VCA) (Discrete VCA)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Analogue Core</strong>:","<strong>Oscillators</strong>: Discrete analogue oscillators (Digitally Controlled Oscillators, or DCOs) driven by high-stability analog core circuits whose pitch is stabilized and controlled via digital micro-controllers.","<strong>Mixer</strong>: Pure analog summing bus combining up to six discrete sources (Osc 1, Osc 2, Sub-Oscillator, Noise Generator, Ring Modulator, External Input).","<strong>Filter Section</strong>: Dual-topology discrete analogue filters consisting of:","<em>Classic Filter</em>: State-variable filter supporting Low-Pass (LP), Band-Pass (BP), and High-Pass (HP) responses at both 12 dB/octave (2-pole) and 24 dB/octave (4-pole) slopes.","<em>Acid Filter</em>: 4-pole, 24 dB/octave diode-ladder topology offering fixed low-pass characteristics with non-linear resonance saturation.","<strong>Amplifier (VCA) &amp; Output</strong>: Analogue Voltage Controlled Amplifier driving a high-current unbalanced line output and a dedicated stereo headphone amplifier stage."],"ordered":true},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Digital Control System</strong>:","Every physical potentiometer, rotary encoder, fader, and toggle switch is scanned digitally.","When a patch is saved, control values are quantized into digital registers within non-volatile onboard EEPROM/Flash memory.","Modulation sources such as the dual Low-Frequency Oscillators (LFO 1 and LFO 2) are digitally generated, enabling ultra-wide frequency ranges (from zero up to approximately 190 Hz), precise MIDI clock synchronization (with 35 discrete rhythmic subdivisions), and mathematical waveshaping/slewing.","Dynamic parameter updating occurs without introducing stepping artifacts into the analogue core through high-speed digital-to-analogue conversion (DAC) pipelines."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Power on the synthesizer while monitoring the 3-character LED display.","Confirm the startup sequence: the display momentarily flashes the installed firmware revision before settling on Patch <code>0</code>.","Rotate the large <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> potentiometer. Observe that as the physical control turns, the <strong>Original Value</strong> LEDs (<code>◄</code> and <code>►</code>) indicate whether the physical position matches, sits below, or exceeds the digitally stored value of the current patch.","When the physical knob crosses the exact value stored in the digital register, both LEDs illuminate simultaneously."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Technical Edge-Case & Warning","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!IMPORTANT] The Bass Station II is not a digital modeling synthesizer (DSP virtual analogue). Audio is never digitized inside the instrument. If the physical audio output is muted or disconnected, no audio data is transmitted over the USB connection; USB carries MIDI and SysEx control data only."}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Can you define the specific boundary between what is analogue and what is digital in the Bass Station II architecture?","[ ] Why do the Original Value LEDs illuminate when turning a potentiometer on a newly loaded patch?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-1-2","title":"1.1.2: Monophonic Core Architecture vs. Paraphonic Mode","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>True Monophony (Default Architecture)</strong>:","At its architectural baseline, the Bass Station II is a monophonic synthesizer.","In standard operation, pressing a single key routes the corresponding Control Voltage (CV) pitch to both Oscillator 1 and Oscillator 2 simultaneously (subject to individual coarse/fine pitch offsets).","A single gate signal triggers the discrete Voltage Controlled Amplifier (VCA) and the Filter/Modulation envelopes.","If multiple keys are struck simultaneously, the instrument applies keyboard priority logic (e.g., lowest, highest, or last note priority depending on trigger settings) to assign a single pitch to all active oscillators."],"ordered":true},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Paraphonic Mode (<code>P-1</code>)</strong>:","Paraphony is a multi-pitch operational state that utilizes the two independent primary oscillators (Osc 1 and Osc 2) to track two separate keypresses simultaneously.","<em>Pitch Allocation</em>: The lower-pitched key is assigned to Oscillator 1; the higher-pitched key is assigned to Oscillator 2.","<em>Shared Processing Core</em>: Both oscillators are summed at the analogue mixer and passed through a <strong>single, shared analogue filter</strong> and a <strong>single, shared analogue VCA</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"table","headers":["Characteristic","True Monophonic Mode (<code>P-0</code>)","Paraphonic Mode (<code>P-1</code>)","True Polyphony (e.g., 2-Voice Poly)"],"rows":[["<strong>Oscillator Pitches</strong>","All oscillators track 1 note","Osc 1 tracks Key A; Osc 2 tracks Key B","Osc 1 &amp; 2 per voice track separate keys"],["<strong>Filter Stages</strong>","Single shared filter","Single shared filter","1 dedicated filter per voice"],["<strong>VCA / Envelopes</strong>","Single shared VCA / ADSR","Single shared VCA / ADSR","1 dedicated VCA / ADSR per voice"],["<strong>Articulation Behavior</strong>","All components articulate in unison","Releasing 1 key leaves the other sounding through the active shared envelope","Independent envelope release per voice"]],"caption":null}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Select an initialized or clean patch (e.g., Patch <code>64</code>).","Press and hold the <strong>Function</strong> button, then double-tap the <strong>Osc 1-2 Sync</strong> key (the upper <code>D</code> key on the keybed).","The 3-character display indicates <code>P-0</code> (Paraphonic Off). Press the <strong>Patch/Value ▲</strong> button to toggle the setting to <code>P-1</code> (Paraphonic On).","Press <strong>Function</strong> to exit configuration mode.","Play a root and a major third simultaneously (e.g., <code>C3</code> and <code>E3</code>).","Verify that two distinct pitches sound. Release the upper key (<code>E3</code>) while holding <code>C3</code>. Note that the remaining tone drops back to Osc 1, and the shared filter does not re-articulate independently."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Technical Edge-Case & Warning","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!NOTE] In Paraphonic mode, if an envelope is mid-decay or mid-release, striking a second note will not re-trigger a separate envelope generator for that note; it will re-trigger or modify the single shared envelope according to the active <strong>Triggering</strong> mode (<strong>Single</strong>, <strong>Multi</strong>, or <strong>Autoglide</strong>)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Explain why paraphony is fundamentally distinct from true polyphony.","[ ] What is the exact panel key sequence required to toggle paraphonic mode between <code>P-0</code> and <code>P-1</code>?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-1-3","title":"1.1.3: Hardware Editions and Documentation Lineage","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The core synthesis engine, microprocessor architecture, firmware structure, and MIDI communication protocols are identical across three released hardware variants:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Novation Bass Station II (Standard Edition)</strong>: Dark blue chassis with light blue accents, standard industrial typography.","<strong>Novation AFX Station</strong>: Custom industrial styling designed in collaboration with Richard D. James (Aphex Twin), featuring custom black-and-purple silk-screening, modified typography highlighting AFX Mode overlay controls, and a custom startup animation.","<strong>Novation Bass Station II Swifty Edition</strong>: Limited custom cosmetic edition featuring distinct aesthetic faceplate artwork."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                      Firmware Engine (v5.0 Core)                       │\n├──────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬───────────────────┤\n│    Bass Station II       │       AFX Station       │  Swifty Edition   │\n│  (Standard Silk-screen)  │   (AFX Silk-screen)     │ (Art Silk-screen) │\n└──────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴───────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["All three variants run the identical core firmware (v5.0 architecture).","All three hardware versions utilize the official <strong>Novation Bass Station II User Guide (Version 5.0)</strong> as their authoritative reference.","Any feature available on an AFX Station (such as per-key overlay editing, extended envelope looping, oscillator error drift, micro-tuning tables, and sub-oscillator detune) is fully present and functional on the standard Bass Station II and Swifty Edition under equivalent firmware."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Inspect the faceplate silkscreening above the keybed.","Identify the secondary black-on-white and underlined labels printed above specific keys (e.g., <code>Mod Wh: Filter Freq</code>, <code>Pitch Bend Range</code>, <code>Sync</code>, <code>Dump</code>).","Note that while AFX Station units visually emphasize overlay assignment parameters directly on the top graphics, the physical button combination (<code>Function</code> + corresponding key) executes the identical internal routine across all editions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Does an AFX Station possess distinct synthesis circuitry or DSP algorithms not found on a standard Bass Station II updated to firmware v5.0?","[ ] Which document is the primary authoritative manual for all three hardware variants?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-1-4","title":"1.1.4: Sister Products and Operational Boundaries","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.1.4: Sister Products and Operational Boundaries","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"1. Technical Exposition","level":4},{"type":"prose","html":"To maintain technical precision, strict operational boundaries must be established between the Bass Station II and related products within the Novation/Focusrite ecosystem:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                         SYSTEM &amp; PRODUCT BOUNDARIES                              │\n├───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┤\n│ Bass Station II           │ Novation Launchkey       │ GForce Bass Station       │\n│ (Hardware Analogue Synth) │ (MIDI Controller)        │ (Software VST3/AU Plugin) │\n├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┤\n│ • Generates analogue audio│ • Generates NO audio     │ • Digital DSP emulation   │\n│ • Monophonic/Paraphonic   │ • Multi-octave DAW keys  │ • Polyphonic options      │\n│ • Hardware DCO/VCF/VCA    │ • Custom MIDI Modes      │ • Software FX engines     │\n│ • Mono Line Out / Phones  │ • USB MIDI control only  │ • Host DAW processing     │\n└───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Novation Launchkey Series</strong>:","The Launchkey MK3/MK4 is a dedicated MIDI master keyboard controller.","It contains zero onboard sound generation hardware, zero analogue filters, and zero audio output jacks.","It cannot be programmed using Bass Station II SysEx patch dumps, nor does it share the discrete DCO/VCF architecture.","<strong>GForce Bass Station (Software Plugin)</strong>:","GForce Bass Station is a software instrument (virtual instrument plugin) running inside a digital audio workstation (DAW).","Although modeled on the original 1990s Bass Station rack/keyboard architecture, it runs entirely on host CPU processing.","It includes software-specific features (e.g., expanded polyphony, software-generated chorus/reverb, digital stereo panning) that do not exist within the physical discrete analogue signal path of the Bass Station II hardware."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["Verify the standalone nature of the Bass Station II: connect headphones directly to the rear <code>HEADPHONES</code> jack and plug in the 9 V DC power supply.","Ensure no USB or MIDI cables are attached.","Play the keybed; confirm complete sound generation, modulation, and filter control without any external host computer, driver, or software plugin running."],"ordered":true},{"type":"heading","text":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","level":4},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the primary functional difference between the Bass Station II hardware and a Launchkey MIDI controller?","[ ] Why must software-only parameters of the GForce plugin never be assumed to exist on the physical Bass Station II hardware?"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Section 1.2 — Box Contents, Power Requirements, and Hearing Safety","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Identify all standard components, accessories, and cables included in the factory packaging.","Specify the exact electrical requirements for external DC power (9 V DC, 500 mA minimum, center-positive polarity).","Analyze the electrical and operational differences between external DC mains powering and USB bus powering.","Operate the three-position rear power switch correctly (<code>ext DC</code>, <code>OFF</code>, <code>USB</code>).","Apply critical gain-staging practices to prevent acoustic trauma and hardware monitoring damage from extreme filter resonance and oscillator clipping."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-2-1","title":"1.2.1: Factory Box Contents and Hardware Specifications","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The factory packaging of the Novation Bass Station II includes the following items:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Novation Bass Station II Synthesizer Unit</strong>:","Dimensions: <strong>457 mm (18.00\") Width × 273 mm (10.75\") Depth × 76 mm (3.00\") Height</strong>.","Mass: <strong>2.48 kg (5.47 lbs)</strong>.","<strong>Switch-mode External Power Supply (PSU)</strong>:","Input: 100–240 V AC, 50/60 Hz.","Output: 9 V DC, 500 mA, center-positive barrel connector (interchangeable regional AC plug pins included).","<strong>USB Cable</strong>:","Standard USB Type-A to Type-B cable (2.0 compliant)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                          FACTORY BOX INVENTORY                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Included Items                 │ NOT Included (Acquired Separately)     │\n├────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ • Bass Station II Synthesizer  │ • Sustain Pedal (¼\" TS momentary)      │\n│ • 9 V DC Mains Power Supply    │ • 5-Pin DIN MIDI Cables                │\n│ • USB Type-A to Type-B Cable   │ • ¼\" TS Audio Cables                   │\n│ • Getting Started Guide / Code │ • ¼\" TRS Stereo Headphones             │\n└────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Inspect the physical dimensions and chassis of the unit.","Locate the serial number barcode label on the underside of the chassis.","Verify that the USB connector on the rear is a full-sized <strong>USB Type-B</strong> receptacle."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Is a sustain pedal included in the factory box?","[ ] What is the exact mass and width of the Bass Station II chassis?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-2-2","title":"1.2.2: External DC Electrical Specifications and Polarity","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The external power input of the Bass Station II requires precise electrical conditions to prevent damage to internal analogue voltage regulators and digital logic boards:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                Barrel Jack Polarity Diagram\n                        ┌─────────┐\n             Negative ──│  ──(●── │── Positive (+ Centre)\n            (Outer Ring)└─────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Voltage Specification</strong>: <strong>9.0 V DC (Direct Current)</strong>.","Under no circumstances should an AC-output power supply or a DC supply exceeding 9 V (such as 12 V or 18 V supplies commonly used for guitar pedals or Eurorack gear) be connected.","<strong>Current Rating</strong>:","Factory supplied power adapter provides <strong>500 mA</strong> at 9 V DC.","The official maximum power consumption rating of the hardware is <strong>9 V DC, 300 mA</strong>.","A replacement power supply must provide 9 V DC with a current rating of <strong>at least 300 mA to 500 mA</strong>.","<strong>Polarity</strong>: <strong>Center Pin Positive (<code>+</code>)</strong>.","The center pin of the 2.1 mm / 5.5 mm DC coaxial barrel connector carries the positive potential (<code>+</code>); the outer barrel sleeve carries ground/negative (<code>-</code>).","Connecting a center-negative supply (common on standard guitar effect pedals) will fail to power the unit and risks tripping internal protection diodes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Take the supplied Novation power supply and inspect the molded label on the adapter casing.","Identify the output rating line: verify it reads <code>OUTPUT: 9V ⎓ 500mA</code> (or higher current rating).","Locate the polarity symbol and confirm that the center dot connects to the <code>+</code> sign.","Insert the barrel plug securely into the rear panel <code>POWER IN</code> socket."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Technical Edge-Case & Warning","blocks":[{"type":"callout","variant":"key","title":"Note","html":"[!CAUTION] Using an incorrect power supply, applying AC voltage, or using an unapproved reverse-polarity adapter voids the manufacturer warranty and can cause irreversible hardware damage to the discrete filter circuits and internal power regulation rails."}]},{"type":"step","title":"4. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the required center-pin polarity for the external DC power jack?","[ ] What is the maximum current consumption of the Bass Station II at 9 V DC?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-2-3","title":"1.2.3: USB Bus Power vs. External DC Power Performance","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Bass Station II features dual power topology: it can draw power either from an external AC/DC mains adapter or directly via the USB 5 V DC bus."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                       POWER TOPOLOGY COMPARISON                             │\n├───────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ Feature / Behavior            │ External 9 V DC PSU    │ USB Bus Power (5 V)│\n├───────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤\n│ Internal Voltage Step-Up      │ Minimal conversion     │ Heavy DC-DC boost  │\n│ Noise Floor &amp; Headroom        │ Optimal (Lowest noise) │ Dependent on host  │\n│ Host Sleep Behavior           │ Stays powered on       │ Enters sleep mode  │\n│ Unpowered USB Hub Reliability │ 100% Reliable          │ High drop-out risk │\n│ Maximum Headphone Output      │ Full dynamic range     │ May distort earlier│\n└───────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) and Audio Fidelity</strong>:","The internal discrete analogue signal path operates on internal bipolar rails (e.g., ±12 V or ±15 V derived via internal DC-DC switching converters).","When powered from the supplied <strong>9 V DC external adapter</strong>, the power rails receive clean, regulated, high-headroom direct current.","When bus-powered via <strong>5 V USB</strong>, the internal DC-DC converter must step up the voltage by a larger multiplication factor. Any high-frequency ripple, ground loop noise, or digital switching chatter from a host computer's motherboard or graphics card can couple into the audio output, resulting in audible high-frequency whine or an elevated noise floor.","<strong>Host Sleep &amp; Power Delivery Limits</strong>:","When powered via USB from a computer, if the host computer enters system sleep or standby mode, the USB bus removes current, causing the Bass Station II to power down.","Pressing keys on the synthesizer while the host is asleep will wake the internal microprocessor momentarily, but <strong>will not wake the host operating system</strong>.","Connecting the Bass Station II to unpowered USB hubs or keyboard pass-through ports often leads to voltage sagging below 4.75 V, resulting in erratic display behavior, unexpected reboots, or failure to enumerate in the operating system."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect the synthesizer via USB to a laptop running on battery power.","Connect high-sensitivity headphones to the rear <code>HEADPHONES</code> output.","Turn the Master Volume to maximum on an initialized patch with no sound playing. Listen carefully to the background noise floor.","Disconnect the USB cable, connect the supplied 9 V DC power adapter, set the rear switch to <code>ext DC</code>, and listen to the noise floor again.","Note the audible reduction in background digital switching noise when using the dedicated external supply."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Why does the official User Guide recommend using the external 9 V DC power supply over USB bus power for critical recording environments?","[ ] What occurs to the synthesizer when a connected host laptop enters system sleep mode while bus-powering the unit?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-2-4","title":"1.2.4: Rear Power Switch Configuration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear power switch is a three-position mechanical slide switch located directly adjacent to the <code>POWER IN</code> barrel jack."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       Rear Panel Power Switch Diagram\n       ┌───────────────────────────────┐\n       │   ext DC     OFF       USB    │\n       │  [ ■ ]      [   ]     [   ]   │  ◄── Slide to left for External DC\n       │  [   ]      [ ■ ]     [   ]   │  ◄── Slide to center for OFF\n       │  [   ]      [   ]     [ ■ ]   │  ◄── Slide to right for USB Power\n       └───────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>ext DC</code> (Left Position)</strong>:","Routes power exclusively from the 2.1 mm coaxial barrel <code>POWER IN</code> socket.","The USB connection continues to transmit bidirectional MIDI and SysEx communication, but draws zero operating current from the USB 5 V rail.","<strong><code>OFF</code> (Center Position)</strong>:","Fully disconnects both power rails.","All volatile internal state (unsaved patch edits in the edit buffer) is discarded.","<strong><code>USB</code> (Right Position)</strong>:","Routes power exclusively from the 5 V DC pin (Pin 1) of the USB Type-B connector.","The external DC barrel jack is isolated and inactive."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect both the 9 V DC adapter and the USB cable to the synthesizer.","Slide the switch to <code>ext DC</code>. Verify the LED display powers on.","Slide the switch to the center position (<code>OFF</code>). Confirm the LED display goes dark.","Slide the switch to <code>USB</code>. Confirm the LED display powers on using USB bus power.","Practice setting the switch cleanly to the center position to avoid accidental over-sliding between power modes."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What are the exact three positions of the rear power slide switch from left to right?","[ ] Does sliding the switch to <code>ext DC</code> disable MIDI data communication over the USB cable?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-1-2-5","title":"1.2.5: Gain Staging, Dynamic Range, and Acoustic Safety","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The analogue signal path of the Bass Station II possesses an exceptionally wide dynamic range and fast transient rise times (slew rates) characteristic of discrete analog VCAs and resonant filters."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[Signal Chain Stages &amp; Gain Accumulation]\n  Osc 1 Level (100%) ──┐\n  Osc 2 Level (100%) ──┼──► Mixer Summing ──► Filter Overdrive ──► High Resonance Peak ──► Post Distortion ──► VCA Output\n  Sub-Osc Level (100%) ┘   (Analogue Saturation) (Pre-Filter Drive) (Self-Oscillation Peak) (Pre-VCA Clip)   (Volume Pot)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Resonance Self-Oscillation &amp; Filter Peaks</strong>:","When the <strong>Resonance</strong> control of either the Classic or Acid filter is raised above approximately 75% (value ~95–127), the filter enters self-oscillation, generating a pure, high-amplitude sine wave at the cutoff frequency.","If the resonance frequency coincides with an active oscillator harmonic, output amplitudes can spike by +12 dB to +18 dB above nominal program material within microseconds.","<strong>Pre-Filter Overdrive and Post-Filter Distortion</strong>:","The <strong>Overdrive</strong> circuit introduces pre-filter harmonic saturation, increasing overall RMS signal density.","The <strong>Distortion</strong> circuit adds aggressive post-filter clipping before the VCA.","Combining maximum mixer levels (Osc 1 at 127, Osc 2 at 127, Sub at 127), maximum Overdrive, maximum Distortion, and high Resonance can produce output voltages capable of driving audio interfaces and headphone diaphragms into severe clipping distortion.","<strong>Operational Safety Protocol</strong>:","Always set the physical <strong>Volume</strong> potentiometer fully anti-clockwise (zero) before powering on the unit, plugging in headphones, or engaging a new patch.","When designing aggressive leads or resonant bass patches, calibrate monitoring gain using an external audio interface meter or hardware peak limiter."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Turn the <strong>Volume</strong> knob to zero.","Connect headphones to the rear <code>HEADPHONES</code> output.","Load an Init patch (e.g., Patch <code>64</code>).","Strike middle <code>C</code> repeatedly while slowly advancing the <strong>Volume</strong> control clockwise until reaching a comfortable, moderate listening level (typically 9 o'clock to 11 o'clock on the dial).","Turn the <strong>Resonance</strong> knob to maximum (fully clockwise). Observe the drastic increase in harmonic peak intensity.","Verify that setting the initial listening volume conservatively prevented acoustic shock during the resonance sweep."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Why can filter resonance sweeps cause sudden, dangerous increases in audio output level?","[ ] What is the standard gain-staging procedure before powering on or auditioning unknown patches?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
